{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>For HaShem your Elohim brings you to a good land, a land of streams of water.</b> In contrast to the place of the wilderness; and likewise, 'a land of wheat and barley and vines and figs and pomegranates' — these are fruits that generate blood; and 'honey' — date honey. And he mentioned here the principal of all foods. And in these three verses he mentioned the word 'land' (ארץ) six times: twice in the first verse — 'for HaShem your Elohim brings you to a good land, a land of streams of water'; and likewise in the second verse — 'a land of wheat and barley, a land of olive oil and honey'; and likewise in the third verse — 'a land in which you shall not eat bread in scarcity, a land whose stones are iron,' and so forth — here are six times 'land' in the three verses. And in the fourth verse he mentioned הארץ ('the land'), which is what he said: 'and you shall eat and be satisfied and bless HaShem your Elohim for the good land which He has given you.' And the intent was to mention 'land' six times corresponding to the six climates (אקלימין) that spread out from it and draw from its distillate; and for the seventh climate he mentioned הארץ הטובה ('the good land') in the definite form. And so our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: there are seven lands, and the intent is the seven climates into which the lower world is divided. And this seventh climate, David of blessed memory praised, saying (Psalms 48:3): ״יפה נוף משוש כל הארץ״ — 'beautiful in elevation (nof), the joy of all the earth' — that is, a beautiful climate, for the meaning of נוף is climate (אקלים), from the language of the verse in Joshua regarding the division of the land (Joshua 17:11): ״שלשת הנפת״, which Targum Jonathan renders 'three palachin' (districts). And what is called אקלים in the Greek tongue is called נוף in the holy tongue. And in each נוף there is a change of air according to its temperament and according to its proximity to the sun; and one who is born in this נף and departs from there and enters another נף will fall ill — but Jerusalem is a balanced and good נף, and all who enter there from the various districts would be strong and healthy and would never fall ill; therefore it is called 'the joy of all the earth.'"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:46:59.554625889Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}